  "Attributes": [
    {
      "Id": 759315,
      "attributeType": "LongString",
      "attributeName": "AIDescription",
      "attributeRelationType": "None",
      "Value": "## 11 Sprite Demo (Atari 2600)\n\nThe \"11 Sprite Demo,\" also known as the \"Venetian Blinds\" demo, isn't a game in the traditional sense, but a fascinating piece of Atari history and a playful jab at a corporate rival. Created by Bob Whitehead and David Crane, it showcases a clever programming technique initially developed for Atari's *Video Chess*. The demo simulates a pair of Venetian blinds, controlled by the joystick to raise and lower, revealing a beautifully rendered sunset scene.\n\nThis seemingly simple program holds a significant backstory. Following the departure of several Atari employees to form Activision, Atari threatened legal action, claiming Activision had stolen proprietary information, including the \"Venetian Blinds\" technique. To playfully refute these claims, Activision presented this demo to Atari's lawyers, highlighting the absurdity of the accusation. \n\nWhile never intended as a full game, the demo's sunset backdrop later found its way into Activision's *Barnstorming*. A testament to ingenuity and a lighthearted victory in a corporate dispute, the \"11 Sprite Demo\" remains a unique and memorable artifact of the early video game era."
    },
